1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Every raffle on-chain dedupes by wallet.
Wallets are free. Agent IDs are not: one per KYC,
non-transferable. So one address is one person,
and that can be enforced on-chain.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:22,000
This is OneDraw. Two draws on BNB mainnet.
Draw one is finished and settled.
Draw two is open right now: ten slots,
and anyone holding an Agent ID can enter.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The entrant list lives on-chain,
not in a screenshot. Four addresses entered,
two won, and you can read all of it yourself.

4
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Opening a draw needs no permission and no fee.
Pick the slots and the window,
and the page gives you the exact calldata.
Nothing is deployed for you: you call the same contract.

5
00:00:46,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Source is verified, exact match.
And look at the function list: no owner, no admin,
no upgrade, no withdraw. It never holds user funds,
and participants never approve a token.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:15,000
A loser cannot claim.
A winner cannot claim twice.
An address with no Agent ID cannot even enter.
Those are the actual revert selectors:
the chain refusing, not me asserting.

7
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:26,000
And the whole check is one public view.
True for a holder, false for everyone else.
Any project can call it for allowlists, airdrops
or one-person-one-vote, without deploying anything.

8
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
OneDraw. Sybil-resistant draws, as a primitive.
